The sieve of Eratosthenes finds every prime up to $n$ by crossing out multiples rather than testing each number for divisibility. Start with every number marked as prime, take the smallest unmarked number, and cross out all of its multiples. Repeat.

## The direct version

::: {#1e6248cc .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def sieve_naive(n):
    if n < 2:
        return []
    is_prime = [True] * (n + 1)
    is_prime[0] = is_prime[1] = False
    for p in range(2, n + 1):
        if is_prime[p]:
            for multiple in range(2 * p, n + 1, p):
                is_prime[multiple] = False
    return [i for i, flag in enumerate(is_prime) if flag]

sieve_naive(50)
```

